<div class="article-body">
    <Fragment set:html={`<div class="callout">
          <h2>Dead Hangs for Grip Strength at a Glance</h2>
          <ol>
            <li><strong>What they train:</strong> support grip endurance. Crush grip and pinch grip need other exercises.</li>
            <li><strong>Dose:</strong> 3 sessions a week on non-consecutive days, 3 to 4 sets, 60 to 90 seconds rest. Stop one or two seconds before your grip gives out. This is common training practice and not a tested prescription.</li>
            <li><strong>When grip fails first:</strong> wrap your thumb, chalk if sweat is the problem, rest a day between hard sessions. See <a href="#grip-fails-first">why your grip gives out before your shoulders</a>.</li>
            <li><strong>Track it:</strong> log each hold with the <a href="/training-log/">dead hang timer</a> and compare with your own earlier holds. For measured grip force, see <a href="/grip-strength-by-age/">grip strength by age</a>.</li>
          </ol>
        </div>

        <h2>Why Grip Strength Matters</h2>
        <p>Grip strength limits performance on many pulling exercises. Your deadlift, barbell row, pull-up and farmer's walk can all end when your hands open. Stronger grip removes that limit and lets larger muscle groups work harder.</p>
        <p>A 2015 Lancet study (Leong et al.) of 139,691 adults aged 35 to 70 in 17 countries found that each 5 kg lower grip strength was associated with a 16% higher risk of death from any cause. That is an association in observational data and not proof that grip training lengthens life. Read more in the <a href="/longevity-research/">longevity research</a> summary.</p>
        <p>Daily tasks depend on hand strength. Opening jars, carrying shopping bags, using tools, turning door handles and gripping handrails all need functional grip.</p>
        <p>Strong forearms and wrists help control the elbow and wrist during loaded movements. Climbers, martial artists and racket sport athletes all rely on grip.</p>

        <h2>Grip Strength as a Biological Aging Marker</h2>
        <p>Grip peaks in early adulthood and declines from midlife (Dodds et al., 2014, a pooled analysis of 12 British studies). The rate depends on age and sex.</p>
        <p>Grip strength is a marker and not a cause. The reason it tracks with mortality is not established. Grip may reflect overall muscle mass, health and activity. This page treats it as a marker and not a cause.</p>
        <p>Resistance training can raise grip strength in older adults. No study shows that raising grip strength lowers mortality risk. Nobody has measured dead hang training for this.</p>

        <h2>Types of Grip Strength</h2>
        <p>Grip strength divides into three categories. Each type uses different muscle groups and serves different functions. Dead hangs train one type directly. The other two need other exercises.</p>

        <h3>Crush Grip</h3>
        <p>Crush grip closes the hand against resistance. Handshakes, gripper tools and squeezing a tennis ball all use crush grip. The finger flexors and small hand muscles generate crush force. Dead hangs do not train crush grip through a range of motion.</p>

        <h3>Pinch Grip</h3>
        <p>Pinch grip holds objects between the thumb and fingers. Picking up weight plates by the rim, holding books and gripping wide objects all require pinch strength. The thumb adductor and finger flexors work together during pinch. Dead hangs do not train pinch grip directly.</p>

        <h3>Support Grip</h3>
        <p>Support grip holds the fingers closed against a load that pulls them open. Dead hangs train support grip directly. Your bodyweight pulls downward while your flexors keep your fingers closed around the bar. Carrying heavy bags, holding barbells and climbing all rely on support grip endurance.</p>

        <div class="callout">
          <h3>Grip Type Summary</h3>
          <ul>
            <li><strong>Crush:</strong> Closing the hand. Trained by grippers and ball squeezes. Dead hangs do not train it directly.</li>
            <li><strong>Pinch:</strong> Thumb-to-finger hold. Trained by plate pinches and block holds. Dead hangs do not train it directly.</li>
            <li><strong>Support:</strong> Sustained finger closure under load. Trained by dead hangs, farmer's walks and bar holds. Dead hangs are the primary training tool.</li>
          </ul>
        </div>

        <h2>How Dead Hangs Build Grip</h2>
        <p>Dead hangs create an isometric contraction in the forearm flexors. Your muscles generate force without changing length. Static holds train the endurance of the finger flexors.</p>
        <p>Bodyweight provides the training load. The load is shared unevenly across the fingers and thumb. Per-finger values are not fixed.</p>
        <p>Progressive overload through duration drives adaptation. Hold the bar for as long as you can control. Stop one or two seconds before your grip gives out. Rest 60 to 90 seconds. Repeat for 3 to 4 sets. Add about 5 seconds to your target hold once you complete every set cleanly. Do this roughly once a week.</p>
        <p>Weight addition extends the progression beyond bodyweight. Loop a weight belt around your waist only after a comfortable and pain-free bodyweight hang of about 45 seconds. <a href="/weighted-hang-guide/">Weighted dead hangs</a> make the hold harder without a longer time on the bar. Alternate between bodyweight and weighted blocks.</p>

        <h2 id="grip-fails-first">Why Your Grip Gives Out Before Your Shoulders</h2>
        <p>In a dead hang your fingers must stay closed against your whole bodyweight, so the hands often tire before the shoulders do. Lifters in training forums report the same thing: grip, not the shoulder, ends the hold. These steps often add time without any extra training.</p>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>Thumb over the bar.</strong> Wrap the thumb around the bar on the same side as the fingers. Thumbs resting on top of the bar often shorten a hold. Use a thumbless grip only on purpose.</li>
          <li><strong>Chalk if sweat ends the hold.</strong> Gym chalk is magnesium carbonate. It dries the skin and increases friction between skin and bar. Use it if sweat, not fatigue, ends your hang. No study of chalk and dead hang time was found. See the <a href="/best-chalk/">chalk guide</a> for liquid and block options.</li>
          <li><strong>Check the bar.</strong> Most pull-up bars are 28 to 32 mm (about 1.1 to 1.25 inches) across. Thicker bars are harder to hold. Compare holds only on the same bar. See the <a href="/equipment-guide/">equipment guide</a>.</li>
          <li><strong>Swap grips.</strong> Some people change one hand to an underhand grip partway through a hold to give the fingers a different load. Learn the technique first in the <a href="/mixed-grip-hang/">mixed grip hang guide</a>.</li>
          <li><strong>Breathe slowly.</strong> Breath holding and tense shoulders waste effort. Breathe steadily and keep the shoulders relaxed.</li>
          <li><strong>Warm up.</strong> Arm circles, wrist circles, band pull-aparts and one or two short easy hangs take 2 to 3 minutes. A warm-up helps you feel ready; evidence that it prevents injury is limited.</li>
          <li><strong>Rest between hard sessions.</strong> Keep at least one rest day between hard sessions and save true max holds for a rested day. Lifters in training forums advise against failing a max hold two days running.</li>
        </ul>
        <p>If your grip fails first every time, the <a href="/grip-strength-by-age/">grip strength by age</a> page shows how to measure grip force on a dynamometer, which separates grip force from hang endurance.</p>

        <h2>Forearm Anatomy</h2>
        <p>The forearm has about 20 muscles divided into anterior (front) and posterior (back) compartments. The finger flexors do most of the work in a hang. Knowing which muscles dead hangs target helps you choose complementary exercises.</p>

        <h3>Wrist Flexors (Anterior Compartment)</h3>
        <p>The flexor digitorum profundus closes the fingertips. The flexor digitorum superficialis bends the middle joints of the four fingers. The flexor pollicis longus controls the thumb. Dead hangs load all three of these muscles together. They form the primary grip engine during a hang.</p>

        <h3>Wrist Extensors (Posterior Compartment)</h3>
        <p>The extensor digitorum opens the fingers. The extensor carpi radialis and ulnaris extend the wrist. Dead hangs do not directly train the extensors. Imbalance between finger flexors and extensors is sometimes blamed for elbow pain; this has not been shown for hanging. Some people train the extensors separately with reverse wrist curls and rubber band extensions.</p>

        <h3>Finger Flexors</h3>
        <p>The superficial finger flexors arise from the medial epicondyle of the elbow. The deep flexors arise from the ulna. Long tendons carry force from the forearm muscles to the fingertips. Dead hangs load these tendons under sustained tension. Build hold time gradually.</p>

        <h3>Brachioradialis</h3>
        <p>The brachioradialis runs along the top of the forearm and flexes the elbow. It is the most visible muscle on the top of the forearm.</p>

        <h2>Dead Hang Grip Training Protocol</h2>
        <p>A simple grip protocol uses 3 to 4 sets of controlled holds with 60 to 90 seconds rest. Start with 3 sessions a week on non-consecutive days and add a session only if you stay pain free. Add about 5 seconds to your target hold once you complete every set cleanly. Do this roughly once a week. This is common training practice and not a tested prescription.</p>
        <p>The <a href="/8-week-program/">8-week dead hang program</a> gives a week-by-week plan.</p>
        <p>Record every set in a training log. Write the date, set number and hold duration in seconds. Review weekly to confirm progress. A lighter week about every fourth week is a common coaching convention and not a tested rule.</p>
        <p>Warm up before grip training with arm circles, wrist circles, band pull-aparts and one or two short easy hangs. A warm-up helps you feel ready; evidence that it prevents injury is limited.</p>

        <h2>Supplemental Grip Exercises</h2>
        <p>Dead hangs train support grip. Add these exercises to develop crush grip, pinch grip and wrist stability.</p>

        <h3>Farmer's Walks</h3>
        <p>Hold a heavy dumbbell or kettlebell in each hand. Walk for 30 to 60 meters. Farmer's walks load support grip with a moving load. A static hang cannot. Start light and add load slowly. Perform 3 to 4 sets after your dead hang work.</p>

        <h3>Plate Pinches</h3>
        <p>Pinch two weight plates together smooth-side-out. Hold for 15 to 30 seconds. Start with two 5 kg plates and progress to 10 kg plates. This works the thumb adductor and finger flexors in a pinch pattern that dead hangs do not train directly.</p>

        <h3>Towel Hangs</h3>
        <p>Drape a towel over a pull-up bar. Grip the towel ends and hang. A towel is thicker and softer than a bar. The hold is harder. Start with 10 to 15 second holds.</p>
        <p>Read the full <a href="/towel-hang/">towel hang guide</a> for progressions.</p>

        <h3>Wrist Curls</h3>
        <p>Rest your forearms on a bench with your wrists hanging off the edge. Curl a light barbell or dumbbell through full wrist range. Perform 15 to 20 repetitions for 3 sets. This isolates the wrist flexors through a full range of motion that dead hangs do not provide.</p>

        <h3>Rice Bucket</h3>
        <p>Fill a bucket with dry rice. Plunge your hands in and open, close, twist and rotate against the rice resistance. Perform 2 to 3 minutes per session. Rice bucket work is popular among baseball pitchers and rock climbers.</p>

        <h2>Measured Grip Strength Values</h2>
        <p>No validated population norms exist for timed dead hangs. Compare your holds with your own earlier holds.</p>
        <p>In a pooled analysis of 12 British studies (49,964 participants), median peak grip was 51 kg in men aged 29 to 39 and 31 kg in women aged 26 to 42 (Dodds et al., 2014). Force in kilograms is not hang time. The two cannot be converted.</p>
        <p>The <a href="/grip-strength-by-age/">grip strength by age</a> page lists measured handgrip force by age and sex.</p>

        <h2>Grip Strength in Daily Life</h2>
        <p>Grip strength matters for everyday tasks such as opening jars, carrying bags and using tools.</p>
        <p>Test your grip at the same time of day under the same conditions. Use either a dynamometer (peak force) or a dead hang (endurance). Track your own scores.</p>

        <h2>Common Grip Weaknesses</h2>
        <p>Identifying your specific weakness directs your training toward the most useful exercises. Many people have one main limiting factor.</p>

        <h3>Weak Fingers</h3>
        <p>Fingers open before the wrist fatigues. The bar slips from the fingertips first. This can point to weak finger flexors. Train finger-specific holds on a hangboard or thick bar.</p>
        <p><a href="/grip-attachments/">Grip attachments</a> like fat grips increase finger demand on a standard bar.</p>

        <h3>Weak Wrists</h3>
        <p>Wrist pain or fatigue appears before finger failure. The wrist bends excessively under load. This may point to weak wrist flexors and extensors. Add wrist curls (both directions) at 15 to 20 reps for 3 sets after dead hang work. Neutral-grip hangs suit people who find overhand uncomfortable.</p>

        <h3>Extensor Imbalance</h3>
        <p>Outer forearm or elbow pain has several causes. Stop and see a clinician if it persists. Some people add reverse wrist curls and rubber band finger extensions to balance flexor-heavy grip training.</p>

        <h3>Thumb Weakness</h3>
        <p>Difficulty gripping thick objects or holding objects between thumb and fingers can point to weak thumb muscles. Dead hangs load the thumb less than the fingers. Add plate pinches, hub lifts and thumbless bar hangs (fingers only) to train the thumb.</p>

        <h2>Frequently Asked Questions</h2>

        <div class="faq">
          <h3>Do dead hangs build grip strength?</h3>
          <p>Yes for support grip endurance. A dead hang loads the forearm flexors under full bodyweight for as long as you hold. It trains crush grip and pinch grip much less. Start with 3 sessions a week on non-consecutive days and add time gradually.</p>
        </div>

        <div class="faq">
          <h3>Why does my grip give out before my shoulders?</h3>
          <p>In a dead hang the fingers must stay closed against your whole bodyweight, so the hands often tire first. Wrap your thumb around the bar, use chalk if sweat ends the hold, and use a standard 28 to 32 mm bar. Keep at least one rest day between hard sessions.</p>
        </div>

        <div class="faq">
          <h3>How often should I dead hang for grip strength?</h3>
          <p>Start with 3 sessions a week on non-consecutive days. Add a session only if you stay pain free. Perform 3 to 4 sets with 60 to 90 seconds rest. Stop one or two seconds before your grip gives out. Add about 5 seconds to your target hold once you complete every set cleanly. This is common training practice and not a tested prescription.</p>
        </div>

        <div class="faq">
          <h3>What grip strength is considered strong?</h3>
          <p>Strong is relative to age and sex. A pooled analysis of 12 British studies found median peak grip of 51 kg for men aged 29 to 39 and 31 kg for women aged 26 to 42. There is no validated dead hang time that equals strong grip.</p>
        </div>

